    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S814A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the penal law, in relation to\nincreasing the penalties for sale of controlled substances on park\ngrounds or playgrounds\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nTo protect the safety of children and their families by providing\nenhanced penalties for the sale of controlled substances on park\ngrounds and playgrounds.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one of this bill adds a new subdivision 14-a to section 220.00\nof the Penal Law to define \"park grounds or playgrounds.\" Park grounds\nor playgrounds are defined as \"the area in or within any building,\nstructure, playing field, or playground owned, leased, or maintained\nby the State or any agency or municipality thereof that is not\ncontained within the real property boundary line of a public or\nprivate elementary, parochial, intermediate, junior high, vocational\nor high school and is used on a regular basis as a recreational area\nfor children and is so designated.\n\nSection two of this bill amends section 220.34 of the Penal Law to\nread a person is guilty of criminal sale of a controlled substance in\nthe 4th degree, when he or she knowingly and unlawfully sells on\nschool grounds, to include park grounds or playgrounds.\n\nSection three of this bill amends section 220.44 of the Penal Law to\nread criminal sale of a controlled substance in or near school\ngrounds, to include the sale of controlled substances in or near park\ngrounds or playgrounds.\n\nSection four of this bill provides that this act shall take effect on\nthe first of November next succeeding the date on which it shall have\nbecome a law.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nCurrent law penalizes persons who sell controlled substances on the\ngrounds of child day centers and educational facilities, but not park\ngrounds or playgrounds.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe State of New York has recognized that drug dealers who target\nchildren are especially dangerous to society and, as such, should be\nsubjected to more severe penalties. This effort began in 1986 with\nChapter 280, which targeted those who sell drugs upon or near school\ngrounds. In 1998, this was expanded to include sales of controlled\nsubstances made upon the grounds of child day care or educational\nfacilities. In 2006, expanded these offenses to include sales of\ncontrolled substances made on school buses. This legislation would\ntake an important next step by targeting those drug dealers who lurk\nin public parks and playgrounds in an effort to prey on our children.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2016: S.994 - Passed the Senate\n2015: S.994 - Passed the Senate\n2014: S.2173-A - Passed the Senate\n2013: S.2173-A - Passed the Senate\n2012: S.7152 - Referred to Codes\n2011: S.7152- Referred to Codes\n2010: S.1568 - Referred to Codes\n2009: S.1568- Referred to Codes\n2008: S.1777- Passed the Senate\n2007: S.1777- Passed the Senate\n2006: S.1197- Passed the Senate\n2005: S.1197- Passed the Senate\n2004: S.1504-A - Passed the Senate\n2003: S.1504 - Passed the Senate\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome law.\n",
